<BaseLayout
  lang="en"
  title="MCP domain-split proxy for REA tool catalogs | rea.run"
  description="Filter REA’s large tools/list by domain groups via a stdio proxy — companion to the schema-size FAQ."
  path="/faq/mcp-domain-split/"
>
  <article class="prose-page mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-14">
    <p class="text-sm"><a class="quiet-link" href="/faq/">← FAQ hub</a></p>

    <p class="badge badge-accent mt-4">FAQ · P1</p>
    <h1 class="display mt-4 text-4xl text-[var(--color-ink)]">MCP domain-split proxy for REA</h1>
    <p class="lede mt-4 !max-w-none">When the full <code>tools/list</code> is too large for your host, filter by domain groups instead of corrupting schemas.</p>

    <h2>Problem</h2>
    <p>REA can advertise a large catalog (community measurements around <strong>~139 tools / hundreds of thousands of tokens</strong>). Strict clients and bounded prefills reject the session — see <a href="/faq/mcp-schema-size/">MCP schema size</a>.</p>

    <h2>Pattern: stdio filter proxy</h2>
    <p>A community MIT project, <a href="https://github.com/ghirardo-giorgio/REA-MCP-Split" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">ghirardo-giorgio/REA-MCP-Split</a>, sits between the MCP host and REA as a <strong>stdio filter proxy</strong>. It:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Keeps a maintainable group catalog (e.g. <code>session</code>, <code>core</code>, <code>native</code>).</li>
      <li>Exposes only the groups you enable — measured slices can drop token cost dramatically (e.g. <code>session,core,native</code> vs full catalog).</li>
      <li>Ships catalog update helpers so the filter stays aligned with upstream tool names.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>This is <strong>packaging / proxy</strong> — not a fork of REA’s analysis engines and not a crack tool.</p>

    <h2>Operator steps (conceptual)</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Install and pin <code>rea-agents</code> as usual; confirm <code>doctor</code> on your host.</li>
      <li>Clone or install the domain-split proxy; point it at your REA entry.</li>
      <li>Enable only the domain groups your session needs; leave heavy native groups off until required.</li>
      <li>Register the <em>proxy</em> as the MCP server in the client (not the full REA server), then reconnect.</li>
      <li>If a needed tool is missing, add its group — do not paste truncated/malformed JSON schemas to “beat” provider caps.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Related cuts</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="/showcase/dsh-mcp-rea/">dsh-mcp-rea</a> — curated thin face + <code>rea_cli</code> escape hatch for hosts that cannot filter tools at all.</li>
      <li>Upgrade upstream <code>rea-agents</code> when schema compaction lands; prefer CLI for one-off heavy jobs.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Compliance</h2>
    <p>Proxying tool catalogs does not change the legal target rule: analyze only software you are authorized to inspect. See <a href="/faq/legal-boundary/">legal boundary</a>.</p>

